    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d546">

                <pb facs="0635" n="377" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg377"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 441.11B74/13</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-04-28">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 28, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 1.30 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2002. Your 1471, 26th.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d546-f1"/> Replying to my
                    representations of 17th instant Foreign Office advises me as follows:</p>
                <pb facs="0636" n="378" xml:id="pg378"/>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>The steamship <hi rendition="#italic">Segurança</hi> was allowed to proceed
                        on April 22, all the consignees having agreed to receive their goods through
                        the Netherlands Oversea Trust.</p>
                    <p>While His Majesty’s Government do not “require” cargoes to be consigned to
                        the Netherlands Oversea Trust, they do accept a consignment in that form as
                        proof that the cargo is intended for <hi rendition="#italic">bona fide</hi>
                        consumption in Holland, and they find by experience that no objection to
                        that course is raised by reputable shippers and consignees.</p>
                    <p>As this practice has greatly facilitated and expedited the release of vessels
                        bound for Dutch ports when brought in or calling for examination, it is
                        hoped that the United States Government will not do anything to interfere
                        with its smooth working in the future.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
